# Hopper — project context

A lane-crossing arcade game in the Crossy Road genre. Ten stages. Hop forward,
dodge traffic, reach the white line before the 90-second clock runs out. Four
lives per stage.

**Vanilla JavaScript. No framework, no bundler, no npm, no build step.** Twelve
`<script src>` tags in `index.html`, in this exact order:

    src/stages.js  src/tiles.js  src/hazards.js  src/sim-core.js
    src/render/iso.js  src/scenery.js  src/render/scene.js  src/roster.js
    src/render/sprite.js  src/render/deathfx.js
    src/render/character.js  src/main.js

The order is load-bearing. `sim-core.js` reads `Stages`' constants at definition
time; `character.js` reads `Iso` at definition time. Adding a module means
editing **both** `index.html` and `MODULES` in `tools/playtest.py`.

## Two rules

**1. The primary renderer is real Three.js/WebGL2.** `src/render/world3d.js`
loads the local glTF/GLB models using the vendored runtime. The classic canvas
renderer remains only as fallback. Serve over HTTP; do not open file://.
Native WebGL modules stay outside the classic headless MODULES bundle.
Read `MINIMAX-HANDOFF.md` and `docs/SPEC-AMENDMENT-002-library-3d.md` first.
Before a new asset integration run:

    python3 tools/gamekit-agent/gamekit.py doctor .

**2. The simulation owns positions, the renderer owns pixels.** They communicate
through state only. A renderer change must never alter a collision outcome; a
simulation change must never require the renderer to know anything new.
